module examples.err_cap_provenance

# plans/M7.md item A, 03-hardware.md §1: "The compiler checks provenance
# transitively — a function that touches MMIO, DMA, or IRQ state must be
# reachable through the owning driver's authority."
#
# `golden/check-capabilities` is this file with one line more: there,
# `BlkDriver.service` calls `ack_vector`, so the helper is reachable
# through the driver's authority and accepted. Here the driver's own
# method returns without calling it, and the same declaration is a fn
# holding hardware authority that no driver reaches.
#
# What "touches" means at this milestone, and why: item C owns MMIO
# access, item D owns DMA and item G owns IRQ binding, so *no hardware
# operation is representable in the language yet*. What a fn can do today
# is hold the authority — take a capability parameter, or evaluate an
# expression of capability type. That is the base of the real rule, not a
# stand-in for it: holding the authority is the precondition for every
# operation C/D/G add, so the set of fns this check admits is exactly the
# set those operations will be allowed in.

@driver
pub struct BlkDriver:
    sectors: u64

    fn service(read self) -> u64:
        return self.sectors

fn ack_vector(read vector: IrqCap[u32]) -> u64:
    return 1
